    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lead muted">A method that produces a number is not the same as a method whose number means something. Validation is the evidence that separates the two — and for peptides, several of the standard parameters behave in ways a small-molecule protocol does not anticipate.</p>

    <h2>What validation is for</h2>
    <p>A validated method has documented evidence that it measures what it claims, within stated limits, reproducibly, in the hands of the laboratory that will run it. That last clause matters: validation is site-specific. A method transferred from a contract lab without a transfer study is a method that has never been run where it will be used.</p>
    <p>The parameters below follow the structure used in ICH Q2 and in most pharmacopoeial general chapters. The names are standard; the acceptance criteria are not, because they depend on the purpose. A purity method for a 98% product and an impurity method for a 0.1% limit need different criteria from the same instrument.</p>

    <h2>The parameters, and what each one actually establishes</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Peptide-specific wrinkle</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Specificity / selectivity</td><td>Does the method respond to the analyte and not to everything else?</td><td>Degradation products can be chromatographically close; a photodiode array scan helps but does not prove resolution of co-eluting species</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Linearity</td><td>Is response proportional to concentration across the range?</td><td>Peptides adsorb to surfaces at low concentration; the low end may curve downward</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Range</td><td>Over what concentrations is the method validated?</td><td>Purity methods need a range around the main component and a separate, lower range for impurities</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>How close is the result to the true value?</td><td>Requires a reference standard of known content; a “98%” standard is itself an assumption</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Precision</td><td>Do repeated measurements agree?</td><td>Repeatability within a run; intermediate precision across days, analysts and columns</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LOD / LOQ</td><td>What is the smallest amount that can be detected or quantified?</td><td>Impurity limits below 0.1% require demonstration, not extrapolation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Robustness</td><td>Do small deliberate changes break the method?</td><td>pH, gradient slope, column lot and temperature are the usual sensitivities</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Solution stability</td><td>How long can prepared samples wait?</td><td>Peptide solutions can degrade or adsorb during a long autosampler queue</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Specificity is harder than it looks</h2>
    <p>For a peptide, the most important interference is not a solvent or a buffer — it is a molecule one amino acid away from the target. A deletion sequence differs by a residue mass and often elutes close to the main peak; an oxidised variant differs by 16 Da and may be only partially resolved.</p>
    <p>Three practical tests of specificity:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Forced degradation.</strong> Expose the sample to acid, base, heat, oxidant and light, then show that the resulting peaks are resolved from the main peak. This is the standard way to demonstrate that a purity method can see degradation products at all.</li>
      <li><strong>Orthogonal detection.</strong> A photodiode array detector shows whether a peak is spectrally pure; mass detection shows whether a peak contains more than one mass. Neither is conclusive alone, but a peak that is spectrally impure is definitely not pure.</li>
      <li><strong>Spike recovery.</strong> Add a known amount of a related impurity or a known degradation product and show that it is recovered and resolved.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>What specificity cannot establish is that everything present has been seen. A species with no chromophore at the detection wavelength is invisible to UV, which is why the impurity discussion in <a href="/knowledge/peptide-synthesis-impurities/">synthesis impurities</a> separates mass-shifted from mass-silent species.</p>

    <h2>Linearity, range and the low-concentration problem</h2>
    <p>Linearity is usually demonstrated with five or more concentration levels, a regression, and a residual analysis. The correlation coefficient alone is a weak criterion: an r² of 0.999 can coexist with a systematically curved low end, and the low end is exactly where impurity limits live.</p>
    <p>For peptides, two effects distort the low end:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Adsorption.</strong> At low concentrations a meaningful fraction of the analyte can bind to the vial, the needle or the column. The result is a negative bias that grows as concentration falls — see <a href="/knowledge/peptide-adsorption-labware/">adsorption to labware</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Integration thresholds.</strong> Software that ignores peaks below a height or area threshold will report better purity at low load than at high load. The setting should be fixed in the method and stated in the report.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The range should therefore be split: one range for the main component (typically 80–120% of the nominal concentration) and a separate, lower range for impurities, each validated on its own. A single 0.1–200% range validated with one regression is a common shortcut that hides exactly the behaviour that matters.</p>

    <h2>Accuracy needs a reference standard, and the standard has uncertainty</h2>
    <p>Accuracy is established by comparing the method's result against a known value — a certified reference material, a standard of assigned content, or a primary method such as amino-acid analysis or qNMR.</p>
    <p>This creates a circularity that buyers should understand. If the “known” value comes from the same HPLC method, the experiment tests repeatability, not accuracy. If it comes from a commercial standard, the standard's assigned content carries its own uncertainty and its own basis (as-is, anhydrous or peptide content). The distinction between those bases is set out in <a href="/knowledge/peptide-net-content-and-cost/">net peptide content</a>.</p>
    <p>A defensible accuracy study states: the reference material, its assigned value and basis, the number of levels and replicates, and the acceptance criterion. “Recovery 98–102%” without those details is a sentence, not a study.</p>

    <h2>Precision has two floors</h2>
    <p>Repeatability (same analyst, same day, same instrument) is the easier number and the less useful one. Intermediate precision — different day, different analyst, fresh sample preparation, sometimes a different column lot — is what predicts whether two shipments will be comparable.</p>
    <p>Report both, as relative standard deviation at a stated concentration. For a purity method on a 98% main peak, the RSD of the main-peak area is usually small; the RSD of a 0.2% impurity peak is much larger, because the peak is small and the integration is less stable. A method can be precise for the main component and imprecise for the impurities that determine whether the batch passes.</p>

    <h2>LOD and LOQ: signal-to-noise is a starting point</h2>
    <p>The common approach uses a signal-to-noise ratio of about 3:1 for detection and 10:1 for quantification, measured on a sample containing the analyte near the expected limit. The alternative is the standard-deviation-of-response approach, based on the variability of blank or low-level measurements.</p>
    <p>Both produce a number; neither proves that the peak is the right molecule at that level. For impurity limits, the LOQ should be confirmed by preparing a sample at the claimed limit and showing acceptable recovery and precision. A method with a stated LOQ of 0.05% that has never been tested at 0.05% has a calculated limit, not a demonstrated one.</p>

    <h2>Robustness is where peptide methods fail</h2>
    <p>Deliberate variation of method parameters shows which ones the separation depends on. For reversed-phase peptide methods the usual sensitivities are:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Mobile-phase pH and ion-pairing agent concentration.</strong> A small shift changes the charge state of basic residues and moves retention.</li>
      <li><strong>Gradient slope and delay volume.</strong> Different instrument plumbing changes the effective gradient at the column.</li>
      <li><strong>Column lot and age.</strong> End-capping and pore structure vary between lots; a method that only works on one lot is not transferable.</li>
      <li><strong>Column temperature.</strong> Retention and selectivity both move; ±5 °C is a common probe.</li>
      <li><strong>Sample solvent strength.</strong> Injecting in a solvent stronger than the mobile phase distorts early peaks.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Robustness is also the parameter most likely to be skipped, because it is the one that produces failures rather than a tidy table. A supplier who can describe which parameters were varied, and what happened, is describing a method they have actually stress-tested.</p>

    <h2>What this means when you read a certificate</h2>
    <p>A certificate reports results. Validation reports the method's capability. They are different documents, and the certificate rarely cites the validation report. Reasonable questions:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Is the purity method validated for this product, or is it a generic gradient applied to everything?</li>
      <li>What is the validated range, and is the reported result inside it?</li>
      <li>What is the LOQ for related impurities, and was it demonstrated at that level?</li>
      <li>How was specificity shown — forced degradation, orthogonal detection, or neither?</li>
      <li>Is the method transferable, and has it been run on more than one column lot?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>None of these require the supplier to hand over proprietary details. All of them require that a validation file exists and that someone can answer from it. Where that is not possible, the honest reading of the certificate is “a result from an uncharacterised method” — which may still be useful, but should not be treated as a release decision on its own.</p>

    <div class="ruo">For laboratory research use only. This article describes analytical practice; it makes no claim about any specific compound, certificate or laboratory, and does not support administration to humans or animals.</div>

    <h2>Editor's take</h2>
    <p>An unvalidated method produces numbers, not evidence. Accuracy, precision, specificity and range are what turn a peak area into a defensible result.</p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>This guide describes methods and interpretation, not acceptance limits. Limits must come from your own specification and, where applicable, the relevant pharmacopoeial monograph. No testing was performed by the author for this page.</p>

